---
title: "Spacing"
description: "Padding, margin, and the space between children, on the spacing scale."
section: Utilities
sectionOrder: 3
order: 3
---

A bare number is a step on the spacing scale (`--p: 4` is `4 × --spacing`). The named `--space-*` tokens map to 1, 2, 4, 6, 11, 24, and 40; use the number in `style`. Any length works too. Sides are `t`, `r`, `b`, `l` and set logical properties, so `l` is the right side in a right-to-left document. Margins also take `auto`.

`--space-x` and `--space-y` go on a container and put a margin after each direct child but the last: on the inline end for `x`, the block end for `y`. They take states and breakpoints on the container and need a base (`--space-y: 2; --space-y--md: 4`). A child's own margin utility wins. A nested container on the same axis still gets its parent's margin; its own value spaces its children. Dialogs and popovers keep their own margins. A hidden last child still counts as the last, so the visible one before it keeps its margin, and `flex-direction: row-reverse` puts the margins on the wrong side. In flex and grid, use `--gap`; it wraps cleanly and leaves the children's margins alone.

